WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:08.820
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.330
I'm Maurice Samuels, the
Director of the Yale Program

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.440
for the Study of Antisemitism.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.520
It's my great pleasure to
welcome you this afternoon

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.630
to the second in our special

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.350
Benjamin and Barbara
Zucker Lecture Series,

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.570
devoted to exploring the connections,

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.930
but also the divergences,
between antisemitism

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.220
and other forms of racism and prejudice.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:33.890
I'm really happy to
introduce today's speaker,

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.740
Cathy Park Hong.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:39.210
She holds degrees from Oberlin
and the University of Iowa,

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.550
and is currently professor
of creative writing

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.440
at Rutgers Newark.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.290
Considered one of America's
most innovative poets,

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.080
she's the author of three
collections of poetry,

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.260
including "Dance, Dance, Revolution",

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:53.430
which was chosen by Adrienne Rich

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.030
for the Barnard Women Poet's Prize.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.660
Among her many other honors,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.480
I'll mention just the
Guggenheim Fellowship,

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.020
the National Endowment
for the Arts Fellowship,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.070
and Yale's own Windham Campbell Prize.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.100
Many of you, no doubt,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.550
remember her electrifying
lectures on campus,

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.640
when she received that prize, in 2018.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.450
Today though, we're here to talk about

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:19.347
Cathy Park Hong's most recent
book of creative nonfiction,

00:01:19.347 --> 00:01:23.450
"Minor Feelings: an
Asian-American-Reckoning."

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.710
This book was released last February

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.020
and has received what can only be called

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.770
a passionate response in the press.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:31.980
Writing in the New Yorker,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.167
the critic Jia Tolentino said,

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:36.250
"Cathy Park Hong's book of essays

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.240
bled a dormant discomfort

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.930
out of me with surgical precision."

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.357
Claudia Rankin said,

00:01:42.357 --> 00:01:46.430
"To read this book is
to become more human."

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.160
So, the plan for today,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.970
is that I'm gonna engage
Cathy in discussion

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.010
for about a half an hour

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:57.210
and then I'll ask her
questions from the audience.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.750
So please submit your
questions to me at any time,

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.030
during the discussion,

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.660
using the Q and A function

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.320
at the bottom of your Zoom screen.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.180
The chat function is disabled

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.810
and audience members will
remain otherwise muted.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.553
So now, please join me in
welcoming Cathy Park Hong.

00:02:19.837 --> 00:02:20.980
Hi, Cathy.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.213
Unmute yourself.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.243
- [Cathy] Oh, okay.

00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:26.076
- Great, okay.

00:02:26.076 --> 00:02:27.050
Well, welcome.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.370
So let me just say that,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.640
I just thought this book
was totally amazing.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.370
It's so smart, so brave

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.410
and I think it's really an important book,

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:40.750
especially right now,

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:42.510
as we engage in this kind of

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.280
collective conversation
about racism in the U.S.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.773
So, thank you so much for being here.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.250
- Thank you so much.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.120
Thank you for inviting me

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.120
and I'm really happy to be present

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.090
and talk to everyone at Yale here.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.580
- Terrific, okay well let's dive in.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.823
So, my first question,

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.700
you begin with an excruciating,

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.670
but also hilarious story

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:15.220
of getting rejected by

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:19.200
and then kind of stalking
a Korean therapist,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.200
when you were depressed.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.490
So, you were sure that she would

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:25.810
understand all your issues,
because she was Korean,

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:26.970
and then you couldn't believe it,

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.200
when she didn't wanna
take you on as a client,

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.700
maybe because of the dozens of voicemails

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.710
that you left on her machine.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.740
So the story immediately made me think

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:36.990
I was gonna love the book,

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:40.150
but I'm curious, if you
could say a little bit more

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.930
about why you chose to put this kind of

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.023
painful anecdote, right at the beginning.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:52.090
- Probably because it was
an excruciating experience.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.150
It's both kind of absurd
and rather abject.

00:03:59.694 --> 00:04:01.940
I think really, I wanted to,

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.680
maybe I started with that anecdote,

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.803
because I also wanted
to disarm the reader.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:14.050
So I think perhaps in maybe

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:17.100
a more conventional kind
of memoir, nonfiction book,

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:21.230
there's an encounter with the therapist

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:25.290
and especially if it's a
therapist of the same culture,

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.800
the same cultural background,

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.790
there is a kind of kinship that's kindled

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:33.790
and then the retelling and
the talking cure begins.

00:04:39.674 --> 00:04:42.120
Instead, in my anecdote,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.523
the therapist just slams
the door in my face,

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:50.580
where I was expecting this
kind of shared kinship.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.080
There was a Korean word for it,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.773
(speaking in Korean)

00:04:53.773 --> 00:04:56.170
instead, she just flat out rejects me.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:59.850
Which I was so insulted
and hurt by at the time.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.823
But in retrospect, I also
find it kind of funny.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.400
So, it kind of actually,

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.560
kind of gave me a bit of
a glee to begin with that,

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.793
to disarm the reader on
what this book was gonna be.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.750
Also, to show that the persona,

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:19.540
it's also I would say
that myself in the book

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:20.960
is kind of persona,

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.573
is not exactly gonna be
this nice, neutral person.

00:05:27.309 --> 00:05:30.540
But besides from disarming the reader,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.473
I think I also wanted to,

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.730
I mean so much of the book
is about my interiority

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:42.250
and being public about my interiority.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:47.250
There is a lot of passages
about mental health

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.410
and that which is still very taboo

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.493
among many Asian-Americans.

00:05:56.717 --> 00:06:00.760
So it seemed appropriate
to begin with my attempt,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:05.760
at least, to find a therapist
and also in the book,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.690
I talk about how finding
a Korean therapist,

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.520
I wanted to find a
Korean-American therapist,

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.280
because I didn't have to
explain myself as much.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.360
The therapist would just look at me

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.078
and know what I was talking about.

00:06:21.078 --> 00:06:25.790
Then I talk about how I
had to explain myself.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.723
This was sort of my own
kind of reckoning was,

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.703
which I resisted as a poet.

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:35.610
it was to kind of explain myself to myself

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.065
and explain myself to people,

00:06:38.065 --> 00:06:40.720
to others in my community, or my family,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.503
my friends and so to speak.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:47.187
For so long, I also resisted
writing this kind of book,

00:06:47.187 --> 00:06:50.530
the kind of book about
Asian-American identity.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.750
I was like, "Well okay,
if I'm gonna really do it,

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:57.750
I'm gonna really dive into
it and just explain myself,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.520
but not to a white audience.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.687
Explain myself to more to people who share

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.740
what I've been through."

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:12.060
More that, of course it's for everyone.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:16.550
But I think those were the
people I had in mind at first.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:17.840
- Right and I would note that

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:19.580
it was actually a Jewish therapist

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.170
who actually you wound up (laughs)

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.142
seeing.
- I know, I know.

00:07:24.142 --> 00:07:28.870
Our friend, we have a mutual friend Gita

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:31.973
and she read an early draft of the book.

00:07:31.973 --> 00:07:33.610
Then when she saw it (laughs),

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.210
when I said, "I happened to
find a Jewish therapist."

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:38.360
She found that to be
the funniest (laughs).

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:41.170
She found that to be hilarious.

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:42.780
She's still my therapist,

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.350
she's amazing (laughs).
(laughing)

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:47.855
- Well, this actually leads
me to my next question,

00:07:47.855 --> 00:07:49.530
which is that I feel like

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:52.310
there are many overlaps between

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:54.690
the Korean, or the
Asian-American experience

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.620
and the Jewish American experience,

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.780
that I wasn't even aware
of, until I read your book.

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:01.710
But one of the big ones I think,

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:03.843
is this idea of self-hatred.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.140
You describe, there's
a very funny passage,

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:08.217
you describe wondering,

00:08:08.217 --> 00:08:09.780
"Who let in all the Asians?"

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:13.170
When you find yourself in
a room with other Asians.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:14.990
It of course made me think of

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:16.750
Groucho Marx's famous line

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:18.560
about not wanting to join a club

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.580
that would have him as a member.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:21.830
So I'm wondering if you could just say

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:25.410
a little bit more about self-hatred

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:30.410
and about how internalized
prejudice manifests itself,

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.723
for you, or for minorities, in general.

00:08:34.563 --> 00:08:36.353
- I mean, I will say that,

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:39.680
writing this book,

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:43.900
there is like a rich
tradition of Jewish writers

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:46.820
and also Black writers who
write about their self-hatred

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:51.500
in ways that are both full of rage,

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:53.920
but also funny and neurotic.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:56.710
So those are definitely the kind of models

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:59.963
that I was like thinking
about in writing about this.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.210
But I think like, yeah, no.

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:08.260
I mean, it's hard 'cause
you grow up with it.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:13.070
I think like what what's very canny about

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:16.760
living in a white dominant society,

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.010
or white supremacist society,

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.400
is that world I would say,

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.647
is that it creates divisions,

00:09:25.647 --> 00:09:28.390
is the divisions that it creates.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.870
It's not between races,

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.200
between ethnic groups within races

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:39.200
and also divisions within
people in ethnic groups,

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.293
within your family and
also within yourself.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.950
Because, to form any kind of coalition,

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:51.123
would be a threat to those who hold power.

00:09:53.043 --> 00:09:55.310
That's probably something that

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:57.100
we're all kind of abstractly aware of,

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.433
but it's really insidious when it works,

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:05.780
when it starts when you're very young

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:10.420
and then like it's drilled into you

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:14.840
and it's hard to kind of separate yourself

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:16.463
from that sensibility.

00:10:17.443 --> 00:10:21.770
Where I grew up in LA and my family spent

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:23.180
a lot of time in Korea Town.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:28.180
It's not like I grew up in
like Wichita or anything.

00:10:31.754 --> 00:10:36.307
But I went to a high
school that was very white

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:42.810
and I also lived in a
neighborhood later on,

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:44.823
that was also quite white.

00:10:46.886 --> 00:10:50.470
Or even like I remember
visiting Orange County,

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:53.800
which has changed a
lot, since I was a kid.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.520
But back then, it was super racist.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.920
I mean, really just
blatantly, blatantly racist.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:03.555
I'd be hanging out with my cousins

00:11:03.555 --> 00:11:06.357
and the white kids would say,

00:11:06.357 --> 00:11:08.497
"Oh look, there's a Korean parade."

00:11:11.223 --> 00:11:12.400
So from the early on,

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:17.400
I knew that being among other
Koreans or other Asians,

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:21.510
was to invite ridicule,

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:24.230
because we were making
ourselves conspicuous.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:26.643
We were just there.

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:33.713
It was the same for any
other marginalized group.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.780
If you're thinking about
Orange County again,

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:43.070
back then, Mexican
Americans were considered

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:47.450
dirty Mexicans, if they went
to beaches or certain pools,

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:51.490
because they were kind
of infecting the waters,

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:54.003
and of course, Black Americans are,

00:11:56.184 --> 00:11:57.070
if there too many of them,

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:59.300
then they're considered intimidating,

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:00.610
threatening and so forth.

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:03.623
But I think for Asians,
it was more ridicule.

00:12:04.487 --> 00:12:05.320
You still see that,

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:07.760
if you see like groups of Asian tourists.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.850
There's this sort of, people tend to

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:11.973
mock Asians and so forth.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:13.940
Even at the high school,

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:17.150
the Asians were very self-aware of that.

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:17.983
There was a group where,

00:12:17.983 --> 00:12:21.250
they called themselves,
"The Asian Invasion."

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:24.340
I think there's, which is like, yes,

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:25.700
it was sort of making fun of it

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:27.480
and also maybe like just taking it

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:30.427
and sort of reversing it and be like,

00:12:30.427 --> 00:12:32.200
"Yes, we're the invasion, so what?

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:33.800
what are you gonna do about it?"

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.230
But I think that Asian Invasion,

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:38.930
is with that nice little rhyme,

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:43.770
is really just, that kind
of encapsulates the threat

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.590
that we pose as a collective

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:51.590
and that we will invade institutions,

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:58.370
or positions of power,
or I guess like most,

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.670
the examples that we
find most often in media,

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.750
is our schools, right?

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:08.000
There's this very kind of

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:11.770
corporatized, institutionalized
notion of diversity,

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:13.610
where it's fine, as long as the majority

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.530
is still pretty white.

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:20.530
Then you have samplings
from other racial groups.

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:23.450
But if you have too many black people,

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:26.353
too many Latinex people, too many Asians,

00:13:26.353 --> 00:13:27.550
then we're a threat.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:30.960
So that's what was happening
with say, Harvard, right?

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.420
Because and there was
that discrimination case,

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:36.500
because there were too many Asians

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:39.273
and of course there's
a Jewish corollary too.

00:13:41.096 --> 00:13:43.260
- I was just, yeah, listening to you.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:48.260
I mean that, Jews had
faced a very similar thing.

00:13:49.087 --> 00:13:50.900
Starting in the 1920s,

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:52.530
that's when they started having quotas,

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:53.550
because all of a sudden there were

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:55.740
so many Jewish students who

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:57.770
started to do well on the tests.

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:01.191
In fact, it was supposedly
that's when they started to

00:14:01.191 --> 00:14:06.191
create the holistic kind
of approach to admissions

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.780
and start grading on character

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.880
and all these things as
a way to exclude Jews

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:13.090
and that's being deployed often again.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:14.440
- Yeah, yeah, definitely.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:17.100
It's like the kind of
well-rounded individual

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:19.340
and like what's the subtext of that,

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:23.230
is that like Asian-Americans are robotic.

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:25.190
Only interested in math and so forth.

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:30.190
So I think it's kind
of colonized condition,

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:35.270
where you just sort of,
where we believe it too.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:38.370
Where if you're with other
people who look like you,

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:42.303
you know you lose your
personhood, your subjectivity.

00:14:42.303 --> 00:14:44.470
So it's better to be separate,

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:46.240
that you don't do that,

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:51.240
rather than forming, having solidarity

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:55.340
and feeling proud that
you're with other people,

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:58.140
who share your culture.

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.513
So yeah, it's a big issue.

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:05.220
- A kind of related point is this,

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:06.870
and another thing that I think links

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:08.360
the kind of Jewish American experience

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:09.640
and Asian-American experience,

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:12.120
is the model minority myth.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.105
So this idea which you brilliantly
deconstruct in the book

00:15:15.105 --> 00:15:18.940
that Asians are expected not to complain,

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:21.450
because some of them
are doing economically

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:25.800
pretty well or are even next
in line to become white,

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:28.660
as someone says to you, in the book.

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:30.300
So I'm wondering if you
could say a little bit more

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:32.250
about that model minority myth

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:34.530
and who's interests it serves

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.683
and how it affects members
of the minority group.

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:41.430
- Oh, I mean, I think it ultimately

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:46.430
serves white Americans, white people.

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.910
Or maybe not white people,

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:54.750
but more just sort of the
kind of racial hierarchy,

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:57.650
that we live in currently.

00:15:57.650 --> 00:15:59.782
But I'm not gonna lie,

00:15:59.782 --> 00:16:03.143
it also benefits Asians
too, in certain ways.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:07.980
But I mean, I go into it in the book

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:11.710
and I think anyone who's even just

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:14.930
have the most superficial understanding of

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:17.450
Asian-American history know that

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:19.960
Asians were used as a counter example

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:21.603
to the Civil Rights Movement,

00:16:26.547 --> 00:16:29.590
that we weren't uppity and that we weren't

00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:31.330
gonna ask for a government handout,

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:33.823
so we work hard.

00:16:34.969 --> 00:16:37.860
It's carried on in the '80s and '90s,

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:39.430
there's like Reagan's notion of

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:43.723
the welfare queen and the welfare mother.

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:49.150
Asian-Americans were those who, again,

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:52.180
just worked hard and worked diligently

00:16:56.799 --> 00:16:58.540
and through grit,

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:00.880
got their kids into universities

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:02.640
and lived the American dream.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:05.270
As I said in the book,

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:06.940
a lot of that's also fallacious,

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:08.980
because Asians who ended
up coming to the country

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:11.240
were like, it was a quota.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:14.870
It was only the most
highly educated Asians

00:17:14.870 --> 00:17:16.250
who were allowed in,

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:18.010
who were Asians who were already

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:20.130
engineers, doctors and so forth.

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:21.870
In fact, I just wanna add,

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:24.730
like actually at least among Koreans,

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:27.210
the ones who were engineers
and doctors and so forth,

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:30.503
came in and they actually
ended more downwardly mobile.

00:17:31.878 --> 00:17:32.730
They came in as doctors,

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:34.240
but they couldn't get the jobs,

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:35.910
because they were Asian immigrants.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:39.170
So a lot of them ended up
like working at a deli,

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:40.610
or driving cabs and so forth.

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:45.610
So, I just wanna kind of add that.

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:49.230
So it's always served white Americans,

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:53.383
because we were used
as this kind of wedge.

00:17:54.323 --> 00:17:57.150
Also I wanna say that,

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:58.670
it's very important to remember that

00:17:58.670 --> 00:18:00.633
our status is conditional.

00:18:03.668 --> 00:18:07.890
We have seen that with
Muslims, right after 9/11.

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:09.810
We're seeing that with those

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:13.533
who are East Asian, during COVID.

00:18:14.414 --> 00:18:16.680
I use the example in the book

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.806
about my friend Prageeta Sharma,

00:18:18.806 --> 00:18:22.390
whose father was president of,

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:24.020
was the first South
Asian president, I think,

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:26.124
for a small university.

00:18:26.124 --> 00:18:28.990
Then once he became president,

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:31.270
he became a target of a lot of

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:35.670
these racist attacks,

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:38.820
because what I emphasize in the book is,

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:41.520
that once you actually gain power,

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:45.290
the model minority myth that helped you,

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:47.160
is turned against you.

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:48.930
Because being assimilated,

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:51.658
is not the same thing as having power.

00:18:51.658 --> 00:18:55.140
He had power and so he
needed to be taken down.

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:56.300
Then Prageeta Sharma,

00:18:56.300 --> 00:18:59.800
who was also a director
at a university program,

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:04.110
was just basically shat on
by her white colleagues.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:08.513
I mean, it was just appalling,
but also not surprising.

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:11.810
However, I think there are also Asians,

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:16.810
of course, who benefit,
older Asian-Americans who,

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:22.610
I have my mother's friends,

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:25.323
some of my mother's friends
are voting for Trump,

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:29.305
because they don't want high taxes,

00:19:29.305 --> 00:19:31.100
or because they think
their stocks are doing well

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:34.450
and who cares about the rest of America.

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:37.450
Then there are also, but for
me, like actually the most,

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:39.780
I hope that this book reaches out to

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:45.640
the Asian-Americans who
were like born here,

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:49.360
who are younger, who have
thrived and benefited

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:51.930
from Neo-liberal America,

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:55.913
those who work for Facebook
or Google, Goldman Sachs,

00:19:55.913 --> 00:19:57.050
or who work as hedge funders,

00:19:57.050 --> 00:20:00.840
many of who think they're
completely untouched

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:04.220
by the racial hierarchy and don't think

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:08.573
and equate a good neighborhood
with a white neighborhood.

00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:11.260
I hope to reach out to them,

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:12.500
because I think they're the ones

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:14.603
who are actually adjacent to power.

00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:19.330
Yeah, so it helps Asians too.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:20.220
- Yeah, yeah.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:21.680
I mean, it's such an interesting

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:25.323
and as you point out in the book and said,

00:20:26.212 --> 00:20:30.830
Asians are probably the
highest income disparity

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:32.260
of any minority groups.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:35.070
So in fact, there are so many

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:37.320
really poor Asians

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:40.392
and it must be so wrong,
- Exactly.

00:20:40.392 --> 00:20:41.815
- To hear-
- well I mean,

00:20:41.815 --> 00:20:44.560
that's where the model minority myth

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:46.580
is actually really quite damaging,

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:49.030
is that there is a huge disparity,

00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:51.100
like where you have

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:57.970
Chinese, Hindu Indian
and Japanese Americans

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:02.330
who are a leap, who are on
the highest income bracket,

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:06.273
but then you have those
who are Southeast Asian,

00:21:06.273 --> 00:21:09.610
who are on the lowest economic bracket.

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:11.440
So it's like the model minority myth,

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:13.653
of course, completely ignores and erases,

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:18.444
say the Hmong community in Minnesota,

00:21:18.444 --> 00:21:22.370
who have really, really struggled,

00:21:22.370 --> 00:21:26.923
or the Cambodians who are in California.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:29.973
Yeah, it's a big issue.

00:21:31.512 --> 00:21:34.320
- So, I'm sure many people have asked you

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:37.547
about this title, "Minor Feelings."

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:42.760
You describe minor
feelings in the book as,

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:45.880
I'm quoting, "The
racialized range of emotions

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:48.090
that are negative, dysphoric,

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:51.540
built from the sediments of
everyday racial experience

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:54.570
in the irritant of having
one's perception of reality,

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:57.910
constantly questioned or dismissed."

00:21:57.910 --> 00:22:01.310
You cite scholars and
writers like Claudia Rankin

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:04.970
as inspirations in your
elaborations of the concept.

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:09.970
But I'm wondering what is
specifically Asian-American about,

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:13.330
or what is specifically
Asian about minor feelings?

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:18.330
Is there something, or does it
apply to all minority groups?

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:25.010
- I think it's like a
flexible kind of effect,

00:22:25.148 --> 00:22:28.390
that I was also borrowing
from other theorists,

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:31.940
like Cyan Nye and Lauren
Berlin and so forth.

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:34.320
I do think that it can apply

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:37.923
to many marginalized groups where,

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:41.771
I mean, where they have
these minor feelings,

00:22:41.771 --> 00:22:46.690
living in sort of a
capitalist, racist system

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:48.960
that promises social mobility,

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:51.786
without actually delivering it to you.

00:22:51.786 --> 00:22:55.620
So when you feel stuck, you
think the onus is on you,

00:22:55.620 --> 00:22:56.640
that it's your fault,

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:58.740
rather than thinking that it's structural.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.490
But yes, I think it's
also very particular.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:06.460
You could say it's also a
condition that I think is

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:09.870
and I have found after
the book was published,

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:14.110
that it really did speak to
a lot of Asian-Americans,

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:15.000
who read the book.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:15.960
I was a little bit,

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:18.430
I was like, "Oh, I hope I'm
not speaking out of turn."

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:23.430
But I think what's
Asian-American about it is,

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:29.450
which may make it different than

00:23:29.450 --> 00:23:31.150
which differentiates it from,

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:33.800
say other racial groups is,

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:38.800
this condition of being chronically
overlooked, dimunitized.

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:46.640
Asians, for instance,
are still not even named

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:49.000
when in the polls, when
their racial breakdowns

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:50.190
were still other,

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:51.410
'cause there's not enough of us,

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:54.890
or we're just like, too many
of us live in California.

00:23:54.890 --> 00:23:56.268
So what's the point?

00:23:56.268 --> 00:23:57.740
(laughs) You know?

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:02.070
But it's not just from the country.

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:07.070
I think for a lot of us, it
also comes from the home too,

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:12.790
where whatever your experiencing,

00:24:12.790 --> 00:24:15.413
your perception of reality is

00:24:15.413 --> 00:24:18.780
dimunitized, overlooked,
not just by America,

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:21.440
but from your family too,

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:24.370
because there's this real
kind of perhaps not all,

00:24:24.370 --> 00:24:26.930
but for some that second generation,

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:31.320
first generation conflict
where parents probably have

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:34.913
more of a survivalist kind of survivor way

00:24:37.180 --> 00:24:38.100
of looking at things,

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:40.850
versus the sense of alienation

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:43.853
that younger Asian-Americans have.

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:48.303
I think so there's this constant,

00:24:49.781 --> 00:24:54.040
I felt that my rage or my melancholy,

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:59.040
or my questions weren't
worthy enough to address

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:02.610
and weren't worthy enough to make public

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:04.640
because of how I grew up,

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:09.640
because there weren't
representations of me in media,

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:11.573
but that's not the only reason.

00:25:12.763 --> 00:25:16.330
I think it's even within the
rubric of racial politics,

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:19.913
I have heard a lot of Asians say,

00:25:21.070 --> 00:25:24.420
like I had a younger student,

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:25.830
I had a student who said,

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:30.830
when there was a lot of
anti-Asian racism, during COVID,

00:25:32.570 --> 00:25:35.580
she's like, "Oh, I just didn't
think it was right for me.

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:38.300
I mean I was talking to my black friend

00:25:38.300 --> 00:25:39.460
and I didn't think it was right for me

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:44.460
to express my experiences of
xenophobia and inequality."

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:49.100
I think there's this kind of shame,

00:25:49.100 --> 00:25:52.673
or sense of insufficiency,
where it's like,

00:25:53.570 --> 00:25:56.280
where they think their oppression,

00:25:56.280 --> 00:26:00.053
is not as extreme as Black
or indigenous people.

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:03.090
So there's this kind of

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:05.692
automatic internalized belittling,

00:26:05.692 --> 00:26:08.450
or even self-gaslighting,

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:10.783
or trivializing of her own concerns.

00:26:12.980 --> 00:26:15.000
This is why I wrote this book,

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:16.160
because I think it's really,

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:19.233
we can't play this game
of oppression Olympics.

00:26:21.674 --> 00:26:23.480
In order to fight injustice,

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:28.173
you have to acknowledge and reckon with

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:33.810
and embrace your own life story

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:35.360
and what you had to go through,

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:39.280
in order to kind of have an allianceship

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:40.610
with other racial groups.

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:42.250
If you kind of deny yours,

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:47.010
or deny that kind of minor feelings

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:48.760
from other people like you,

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:52.750
then I don't think anything
can really be done.

00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:54.500
I think it's very important.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:57.250
I mean, I'm sorry, I'm
going on a little bit,

00:26:57.250 --> 00:26:59.980
but I see some Asian-Americans who say,

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:02.265
like to call out other Asian-Americans as,

00:27:02.265 --> 00:27:04.340
"You're being anti-Black.

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:05.720
Stop being so anti-Black."

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:09.610
I'm like, "I don't think
scolding is gonna work.

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:12.300
I think you need to first talk about

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:15.780
reconciling with what you went through,

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:20.110
that you have been taught
to deny and suppress

00:27:20.110 --> 00:27:23.370
and open that up and only
through opening that up,

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:24.810
opening up your vulnerability,

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:27.320
will you really be able to fight

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:31.720
for a more equitable country."

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:34.020
- I think that's what Jia Tolentino,

00:27:34.020 --> 00:27:37.770
I imagine she meant, that
you kind of pointed to,

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:39.450
you point throughout the book, I think,

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:42.640
to these feelings that,

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:44.600
people, not just Asians, I think.

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:48.320
I think this is really valid
for all minority groups,

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:51.960
but these sensations and
things that you've thought of,

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:55.580
but didn't know were a
thing kind of, you know?

00:27:55.580 --> 00:27:57.050
I think by articulating that,

00:27:57.050 --> 00:28:00.130
you just do a huge service to people.

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:02.010
So I wanna encourage people to

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:05.050
please send me questions in the Q and A.

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:07.950
I have a lot more questions,
so I'll keep going,

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:09.990
but please do send me your questions

00:28:09.990 --> 00:28:11.300
and I'll start asking them in a minute.

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:15.096
But I wanted to pick
up on that question of

00:28:15.096 --> 00:28:18.560
the relationship between
Asians and Blacks.

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:22.650
You have one of the most
painful sections of the book,

00:28:22.650 --> 00:28:26.810
is your description of the
Rodney King Riots in LA,

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:30.070
in the early '90s and of the tensions,

00:28:30.070 --> 00:28:33.320
especially between Koreans
and Korean-Americans

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:36.730
and Black people in that moment.

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:39.180
I'm wondering if you could just
say a little bit more about

00:28:39.180 --> 00:28:43.470
where you see Asians fitting in

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:45.960
to the American racial system today

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.490
and especially at this moment,

00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:51.130
of kind of renewed debate and questioning

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:52.613
of those hierarchies.

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:56.110
- Oh, it's very complex.

00:28:56.110 --> 00:28:59.300
I think that there isn't enough discourse

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:02.823
between minorities about,

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:11.860
reconciling or just
addressing the anti-Blackness

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:13.780
within our communities.

00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:17.610
Make no mistake, a lot
of Asians are anti-Black.

00:29:17.610 --> 00:29:22.509
Now granted, this was more
older Asian-Americans.

00:29:22.509 --> 00:29:24.640
I think, there's been a real C change,

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:28.300
which I find to be really
gratifying, actually,

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:29.280
I have to say.

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:33.260
Maybe this is just like the
people that I am exposed to,

00:29:33.260 --> 00:29:36.123
but I will say just from experience,

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:39.970
Asian-Americans are,

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:41.950
at least like Millennials, Gen Z

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:47.800
and maybe some Gen X,
are much more galvanized,

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:53.470
much more activist, much
more aware and woke,

00:29:53.590 --> 00:29:55.210
I guess, is the term,

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:57.250
to racial politics in this country

00:29:57.250 --> 00:30:02.250
and really trying to fight the kind of

00:30:04.109 --> 00:30:07.900
the colorism and the racism
within their communities.

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:10.512
I think we've gone far away.

00:30:10.512 --> 00:30:13.850
We've come a far, far way, far,

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:18.850
since the LA Riots, when
there was this kind of

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:22.660
conflict between Koreans
and Black Americans.

00:30:22.660 --> 00:30:25.870
Yet, we still have far to go.

00:30:25.870 --> 00:30:26.880
I think the LA Riots,

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:29.230
a lot of people have
forgotten the LA Riots,

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:32.640
but I think it's still there.

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:35.950
I mean, the wounds have not healed.

00:30:35.950 --> 00:30:38.670
I think like when a lot
of African-Americans

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:40.583
think about relations,

00:30:41.783 --> 00:30:45.030
Black/Asian relations,

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:49.420
they bring up the LA Riots a lot

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:54.420
and about like how we were
hostile to the Black community.

00:31:02.420 --> 00:31:05.570
It's a lot of work that we need to do,

00:31:05.570 --> 00:31:08.040
but I think it's also
tricky for Asian-Americans,

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:10.600
because it's like, we're both,

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:11.500
as I said in the book,

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:13.653
we're in this purgatorial position where,

00:31:15.326 --> 00:31:19.340
we're both oppressors in a
way, but we're also oppressed.

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:22.369
A lot of the ways in
which we are oppressed,

00:31:22.369 --> 00:31:26.427
they're not seen ever, so.

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:32.260
- Again, I feel like
that's a real parallel

00:31:32.260 --> 00:31:34.070
with the Jewish American experience,

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:35.920
especially historically.

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:38.280
Like early in the century,

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:40.950
many Jews were kind of forced,

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:45.500
because of prejudice,
into an economic niche,

00:31:45.500 --> 00:31:48.490
where they were dealing
with minority groups

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:50.613
and it wasn't always rosy.

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:56.167
It's just complicated levels of racism,

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:01.830
that go into those relations.

00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:05.170
So I think you really do
a great job in I think

00:32:05.170 --> 00:32:09.280
honestly confronting that,
as a way to move beyond it.

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:12.543
So here's a question from,

00:32:14.648 --> 00:32:16.900
we have a couple of
questions from the listeners.

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:18.287
So one is,

00:32:18.287 --> 00:32:19.610
"If you could talk a little bit about

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:22.670
your research process, writing the book?"

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:25.210
This questioner was
really struck by the way

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:28.500
you were able to blend and contextualize

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:30.102
your own experiences,

00:32:30.102 --> 00:32:34.040
both with history and
with writers before you.

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:39.040
So you, especially Asian
writers who you do a great job,

00:32:39.932 --> 00:32:42.663
bringing attention to in the book.

00:32:46.297 --> 00:32:48.357
- It was really like (laughs),

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:50.960
writing the book was,

00:32:50.960 --> 00:32:53.020
I have to say, I was a little blindfolded,

00:32:53.020 --> 00:32:57.310
because just kind of working on the book,

00:33:01.790 --> 00:33:03.940
because I've never written non-fiction.

00:33:03.940 --> 00:33:05.350
I mean, I've written reviews

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:09.190
and a couple essays, previously.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:12.440
I guess I did have some
experience reporting,

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:13.920
but like I've never really written,

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:15.640
I'm a poet and I've never written

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:17.110
a book of nonfiction before.

00:33:17.110 --> 00:33:20.820
So it was that.

00:33:20.820 --> 00:33:23.160
Also, I wasn't really sure,

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:25.200
if any of the essays
were gonna come together.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:27.730
So it was actually
really kind of terrifying

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:29.960
to write this book, 'cause it was all

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:32.640
really uncharted territory for me.

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:36.100
So it wasn't like I had this outline

00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:37.670
of what the book was gonna be like.

00:33:37.670 --> 00:33:41.673
I had these set of concerns
that I was gonna research.

00:33:42.734 --> 00:33:47.270
What I did was, I just kind
of followed my obsessions.

00:33:48.980 --> 00:33:53.980
I followed what I felt
compelled by, my compulsions.

00:34:02.435 --> 00:34:07.270
I just hoped that my obsessions

00:34:07.270 --> 00:34:09.620
would just kind of fall,

00:34:09.620 --> 00:34:12.913
would just all kind of
neatly come together.

00:34:15.171 --> 00:34:18.837
So I began, 'cause I wasn't thinking,

00:34:18.837 --> 00:34:22.280
"I'm gonna write a book about
the Asian-American condition."

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:23.270
I did not think that.

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:25.950
It was originally gonna be a book,

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:27.880
when I conceived as a book of essays,

00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:31.170
I was like, it was vaguer than that.

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:35.167
It was about race and institutional racism

00:34:35.167 --> 00:34:39.010
and the arts, portrait of an artist,

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:42.400
my experience as a poet.

00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:43.890
It was just sort of these

00:34:43.890 --> 00:34:45.883
kind of vague ideas that I had.

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:48.960
With each chapter, it was a subject

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:51.380
that I just couldn't stop thinking about.

00:34:51.380 --> 00:34:54.270
So we know with this book,

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:59.270
I began with the friendship essay,

00:34:59.390 --> 00:35:01.670
which had maybe less research

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:03.970
and more to do with just kind of

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:07.753
really going back to that
time and remembering.

00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:14.450
Then the Richard Pryor was also,

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:20.150
me researching standup comedy

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:21.810
and watching a lot of standup

00:35:21.810 --> 00:35:23.640
and watching a lot of Richard Pryor,

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:26.570
which research that I had already done,

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:28.220
before working on this book,

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:30.850
because I was already
obsessed with Richard Pryor

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:34.660
and had all of this transcribed material,

00:35:34.660 --> 00:35:37.270
during a period where I had writer's block

00:35:37.270 --> 00:35:38.710
and I was like, "All right, I'm just gonna

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:41.440
transcribe Richard Pryor,
as if I was going to be

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:43.077
his apprentice or something."

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:46.470
I just didn't know what to do with it.

00:35:46.470 --> 00:35:50.040
I had all of this sort
of research on comedy

00:35:50.040 --> 00:35:51.807
and Richard Pryor and I was like,

00:35:51.807 --> 00:35:54.320
"Maybe I could turn it
into poetry or something."

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:59.320
Instead, it just ended
up becoming this essay.

00:36:01.390 --> 00:36:04.040
Then Theresa Hak Kyung Cha,

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:06.993
was another kind of obsession that I had.

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:11.850
I didn't actually want to work on that.

00:36:11.850 --> 00:36:13.250
I didn't wanna do it, actually.

00:36:13.250 --> 00:36:18.250
I was like, "This is too hard."

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:20.990
- Well, it's a very painful
episode in the book.

00:36:20.990 --> 00:36:22.480
I mean, for those who haven't read it,

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:24.050
I don't know if you wanna just say?

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:29.050
- Yeah, it's an essay
about this poet/artist,

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:31.930
Theresa Hak Kyung Cha,

00:36:31.930 --> 00:36:36.590
who was raped and
murdered in New York City,

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:38.430
in the early '80s.

00:36:38.430 --> 00:36:43.110
I was really influenced
by her book, "Dictee",

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:45.080
when I was in college and have always been

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:47.710
influenced by her since then.

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:49.243
What I discovered was,

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:54.000
that no one has written
about her rape and murder.

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:56.210
So the book is, of the essay,

00:36:56.210 --> 00:36:58.570
it's kind of like a true crime essay,

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:00.800
but it's also just more an investigation

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:04.030
about why the silence,
why no one wrote about it,

00:37:04.030 --> 00:37:07.430
both among Asian-Americans and scholars

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:10.740
and also media at the time and so forth.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:12.767
It was me kind of
digging into that silence

00:37:12.767 --> 00:37:15.660
and also kind of giving myself
permission to write about it.

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:18.120
But I didn't wanna
write about it at first,

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:23.120
because I was like, "This is
too depressing of a subject."

00:37:24.470 --> 00:37:25.870
I feel weird writing about it,

00:37:25.870 --> 00:37:28.400
since like maybe the family
doesn't wanna talk about it

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:32.680
and it wasn't fun to write,

00:37:32.680 --> 00:37:37.210
but I just felt compelled,
I had to write it.

00:37:37.210 --> 00:37:39.560
So like I had these several essays

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:43.440
and then I just started realizing like

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:46.590
the kind of threads
started becoming visible.

00:37:46.590 --> 00:37:50.970
It was like art making capitalism,

00:37:50.970 --> 00:37:53.650
of course Asian-American
identity and so forth.

00:37:53.650 --> 00:37:56.420
Then I started kind of shaping the essays,

00:37:56.420 --> 00:37:59.660
so that they really had
more to do with that.

00:37:59.660 --> 00:38:02.370
They kind of had more of a through line,

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:04.530
but I think when I was researching,

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:08.973
I was researching each
essay individually, yeah.

00:38:08.973 --> 00:38:11.950
- I really feel like your feeling

00:38:11.950 --> 00:38:13.610
when you called it a compulsion.

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:16.140
I think you can really
feel that in the book,

00:38:16.140 --> 00:38:18.730
that these things you're
writing about them,

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:22.720
because you can't not write about them.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:25.570
This actually relates to another question

00:38:25.570 --> 00:38:27.865
from someone in the
audience, which is that,

00:38:27.865 --> 00:38:30.310
"In the book you speak directly about

00:38:30.310 --> 00:38:32.740
many sensitive topics like suicide,

00:38:32.740 --> 00:38:35.663
but I would add also, rape and murder.

00:38:36.738 --> 00:38:38.280
So how do you navigate the boundary

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:42.739
between processing and re-traumatization

00:38:42.739 --> 00:38:44.520
and how you talk about certain incidents?"

00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:49.250
I think the suicide, there's also,

00:38:50.870 --> 00:38:52.740
I'm thinking of the friendship chapter

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:54.640
where you talk about your
two friends in college

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:56.980
and one of whom turns out to have

00:38:56.980 --> 00:39:01.163
like pretty serious addiction
and mental health issues.

00:39:02.130 --> 00:39:03.830
So anyway, if you could
say a little bit about

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:05.433
how you navigate that boundary.

00:39:07.090 --> 00:39:08.650
- I think part of it is by being

00:39:08.650 --> 00:39:11.661
as self-aware as possible and
as considerate as possible

00:39:11.661 --> 00:39:15.430
and asking myself as many questions,

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:16.838
as I'm writing about it.

00:39:16.838 --> 00:39:21.838
I think, I don't know if I'm interpreting

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:27.710
your term re-traumatization,
because I'm wondering,

00:39:27.970 --> 00:39:30.930
is it re-traumatizing the reader?

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:32.600
- It's someone asking from the audience,

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:35.428
so I don't know exactly what she meant.

00:39:35.428 --> 00:39:40.428
But I think what she means is,

00:39:42.250 --> 00:39:47.250
by writing about this are you,

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:49.960
I don't know if she's
talking about re-traumatizing

00:39:49.960 --> 00:39:51.860
in the person who might read it,

00:39:51.860 --> 00:39:53.960
or anyone who has experienced

00:39:53.960 --> 00:39:56.030
a similar thing who might get triggered

00:39:56.030 --> 00:39:58.160
and therefore re-traumatized.

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:01.530
- Yeah, no, I mean, I think
that's a good question.

00:40:01.530 --> 00:40:03.313
I mean, that was always on my mind.

00:40:05.031 --> 00:40:08.950
Also, in the book I say, not only that,

00:40:08.950 --> 00:40:12.630
I say, I also criticize
narratives of trauma.

00:40:12.630 --> 00:40:14.940
Like who does this really benefit?

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:15.950
Is it really kind of,

00:40:15.950 --> 00:40:17.993
is it gonna really also,

00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:23.760
is it edifying or
empowering for the person,

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:25.970
for the group who had
this collective trauma,

00:40:25.970 --> 00:40:27.792
or is this for the white audience?

00:40:27.792 --> 00:40:30.272
I'm like, "Am I doing the same thing,

00:40:30.272 --> 00:40:35.272
by writing about rape
and suicide and murder?"

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:39.414
But I think also,

00:40:39.414 --> 00:40:42.010
and maybe this is an easy out,

00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:44.580
but I also say, "Like,
I think writing about

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:48.710
any racial condition is gonna
be full of contradictions

00:40:49.550 --> 00:40:53.470
and you just have to be open to it."

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:57.360
I think part of it was being very aware,

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:59.810
but I also had to push forward with it,

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:04.633
because like the case of
Theresa Hak Kyung Cha,

00:41:05.470 --> 00:41:08.490
the reason why no one wrote about it was,

00:41:08.490 --> 00:41:11.433
because of this fear of re-traumatization.

00:41:13.668 --> 00:41:16.541
I also talk about this, right,

00:41:16.541 --> 00:41:20.220
in the essay about Theresa Hak Kyung Cha,

00:41:20.220 --> 00:41:25.220
where I say, at least
in certain communities,

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:27.350
the Asian-American community,

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:28.780
there is such a,

00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:31.823
not to prioritize Western
thinking or something,

00:41:32.826 --> 00:41:33.926
but in psychoanalysis,

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:41.610
the whole kind of basic tenant of

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:45.400
the talking cure is that
once you talk about it,

00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:47.320
it no longer afflicts you, right?

00:41:47.320 --> 00:41:52.210
It's no longer something that you feel

00:41:53.520 --> 00:41:55.450
subconsciously oppressed by.

00:41:55.450 --> 00:41:58.947
Whereas, within my family,

00:42:01.230 --> 00:42:02.760
the kind of family I grew up in,

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:04.983
you couldn't talk about your pain,

00:42:06.030 --> 00:42:11.030
because if you did, you not
only re-traumatized yourself,

00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:14.267
but you re-traumatized
everyone around you.

00:42:14.267 --> 00:42:18.410
You re-traumatized your mother,
your father and so forth.

00:42:18.410 --> 00:42:20.830
Also, there was this
element of shame, right?

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:23.093
That by talking about,

00:42:25.100 --> 00:42:28.980
let's say being sexually
assaulted or something,

00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:32.730
then I was bringing
shame, by saying it aloud.

00:42:32.730 --> 00:42:36.650
I really, really needed
to push against that,

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:38.100
because I think it's still,

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:40.900
that kind of culture is still very strong.

00:42:40.900 --> 00:42:43.053
At the risk of re-traumatizing,

00:42:45.199 --> 00:42:47.030
I think the fear of
re-traumatizing someone,

00:42:47.030 --> 00:42:48.875
can also be very, very silencing

00:42:48.875 --> 00:42:53.593
and can be even more
damaging than helpful, yeah.

00:42:55.360 --> 00:43:00.177
- So another question from an
audience member about genre.

00:43:00.177 --> 00:43:04.840
"So what genre do you
consider the book to be?

00:43:04.840 --> 00:43:08.160
Because you've incorporated
the words of other artists,

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:11.000
your own personal life,
Asian-American history.

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:14.750
It's not a typical memoir autobiography."

00:43:14.750 --> 00:43:18.430
I would just add to that,
what did your editor say?

00:43:18.430 --> 00:43:23.430
So did your editor know
what you were gonna deliver

00:43:23.720 --> 00:43:25.330
and did they try to push you to write

00:43:25.330 --> 00:43:28.560
some more conventional autobiography?

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:29.862
No, okay so.

00:43:29.862 --> 00:43:31.030
- No, I had it.

00:43:31.030 --> 00:43:33.513
Well, actually, yes,
actually (laughs) yes.

00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:36.573
I had a great editor.

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:39.987
I was fortunate to have an editor,

00:43:39.987 --> 00:43:43.730
an Asian-American editor, Victory Matsui.

00:43:43.730 --> 00:43:46.430
Victory's half Japanese,
gender non-binary.

00:43:46.430 --> 00:43:51.430
They were really very tough, very good.

00:43:51.700 --> 00:43:54.370
They didn't want me to
write anything conventional.

00:43:54.370 --> 00:43:55.203
I was very fortunate.

00:43:55.203 --> 00:43:56.598
No one did.

00:43:56.598 --> 00:44:00.650
Not my agent, or my editor,

00:44:00.650 --> 00:44:03.383
or the editor I had after Victory.

00:44:04.792 --> 00:44:07.290
I think that I didn't really care

00:44:07.290 --> 00:44:10.613
that it stuck to any kind of,

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:13.500
I didn't want to write a memoir.

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:14.950
I didn't wanna write a history book.

00:44:14.950 --> 00:44:19.950
I didn't want to write a self-help book.

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:23.595
I wasn't thinking about that.

00:44:23.595 --> 00:44:28.595
I wanted to, I think I just
instinctively interdisciplinary.

00:44:30.573 --> 00:44:33.060
That's how I always was as a poet,

00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:35.160
where I like was not interested in

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:36.300
just writing verse poetry.

00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:39.340
I wanted to write prose poems

00:44:39.340 --> 00:44:44.340
and I would play with science
fiction genres and stuff.

00:44:46.482 --> 00:44:48.100
It was the same with nonfiction.

00:44:48.100 --> 00:44:51.000
Like for me, it was more interesting

00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:54.990
to write about my experience using

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:59.660
a variety of genres, 'cause I needed that.

00:44:59.660 --> 00:45:01.834
I needed those different
systems of knowledge

00:45:01.834 --> 00:45:05.373
to make my argument clear.

00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:08.910
Victory was encouraging of that.

00:45:08.910 --> 00:45:10.880
I would say in the
beginning, in earlier drafts,

00:45:10.880 --> 00:45:15.050
the book was more, a
little bit more academic.

00:45:15.050 --> 00:45:19.623
A little bit more
critical, more impersonal.

00:45:20.783 --> 00:45:24.080
So actually, Victory
did push, pressure me,

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:28.120
I mean, encourage me, to be more personal

00:45:28.120 --> 00:45:31.030
and more autobiographical.

00:45:31.030 --> 00:45:33.660
I think it was more for sort
of the reader's experience,

00:45:33.660 --> 00:45:35.120
but I'm glad that Victory did,

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:37.675
because I thought like by
making it more personal,

00:45:37.675 --> 00:45:40.083
there was more at stake.

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:43.340
I think the book is
much more valuable to me

00:45:43.340 --> 00:45:46.500
and I hope valuable to
readers, because of that,

00:45:46.500 --> 00:45:47.841
yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:47.841 --> 00:45:52.797
I could see those traces of the academic.

00:45:52.797 --> 00:45:56.270
You've read your critical
race theory and it shows,

00:45:56.270 --> 00:45:59.310
but you're not just rehashing that.

00:45:59.310 --> 00:46:00.410
I think that's really

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:01.961
important too.
- Yeah, I mean,

00:46:01.961 --> 00:46:05.190
I had to write a book
that was very much me.

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:07.390
I mean and where I was coming from,

00:46:07.390 --> 00:46:09.700
which is like, I come
from a poetry background.

00:46:09.700 --> 00:46:13.740
I read critical race theory,
but not as a scholar.

00:46:13.740 --> 00:46:15.750
It's not a scholarly book either.

00:46:15.750 --> 00:46:18.150
I read history, I read randomly.

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:21.460
So it's gonna be a book that is

00:46:21.460 --> 00:46:25.733
an assemblage of my random reading habits.

00:46:26.998 --> 00:46:30.053
It's interesting, like
when this book ended up,

00:46:30.053 --> 00:46:32.520
like in the summer, ended up on some

00:46:32.520 --> 00:46:35.770
anti-racist reading list
and I was really glad

00:46:35.770 --> 00:46:40.240
that people were reading
it alongside Ibram Kendi

00:46:44.105 --> 00:46:48.380
and Ta-Nehisi Coates and all
those incredible writers.

00:46:48.380 --> 00:46:50.757
But I was a little bit, I was like,

00:46:50.757 --> 00:46:53.047
"This is a idiosyncratic book."

00:46:54.035 --> 00:46:56.840
I think some people, some readers

00:46:56.840 --> 00:46:58.960
had problems with that
who were expecting more of

00:46:58.960 --> 00:47:03.020
a kind of broader kind of overview

00:47:03.020 --> 00:47:04.840
on Asian-American history,

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:07.383
or kind of like a sort of,

00:47:09.064 --> 00:47:11.740
or Robin DiAngelo's like,
"Self-help: What Do You Do?"

00:47:11.740 --> 00:47:15.920
I'm like, "It was gonna
be a strange little book

00:47:15.920 --> 00:47:17.217
and it still is."

00:47:20.357 --> 00:47:23.530
A lot of it is just my
personal experiences

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:25.660
that clearly does not conform to

00:47:25.660 --> 00:47:29.660
whatever the broad
Asian-American experience is, so.

00:47:29.660 --> 00:47:31.146
- So here's a question.

00:47:31.146 --> 00:47:34.010
I'm interested in how this book relates to

00:47:34.010 --> 00:47:39.010
your day job as a poet and a
professor of creative writing.

00:47:39.600 --> 00:47:43.330
So how have you negotiated

00:47:43.330 --> 00:47:46.950
writing about racial issues in the past?

00:47:46.950 --> 00:47:48.090
You talk about it in the book,

00:47:48.090 --> 00:47:49.160
but maybe you can say something

00:47:49.160 --> 00:47:52.210
and also what you don't really
say in the book is like,

00:47:52.210 --> 00:47:54.480
has this changed your poetry now?

00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:58.500
Like have you gone back and
started writing poetry again?

00:47:58.500 --> 00:48:01.340
Is it any different for
having written this book?

00:48:01.340 --> 00:48:03.970
- I haven't been writing that much poetry.

00:48:03.970 --> 00:48:05.900
I guess when you go
pros, you don't go back.

00:48:05.900 --> 00:48:08.563
Nah, I'm just kidding.
(laughing)

00:48:09.803 --> 00:48:13.150
I think I have been writing some poems.

00:48:13.150 --> 00:48:16.750
I think I, (sighs),

00:48:17.830 --> 00:48:18.663
when I was writing poetry,

00:48:18.663 --> 00:48:20.613
it wasn't autobiographical.

00:48:22.778 --> 00:48:25.936
I wrote poetry like a fiction writer.

00:48:25.936 --> 00:48:28.920
I had lots of different
characters and invented worlds.

00:48:28.920 --> 00:48:30.470
It was a lot of world-building.

00:48:31.320 --> 00:48:36.320
I think after writing "Minor
Feelings", it's opened me up.

00:48:38.304 --> 00:48:41.270
I think I'm much more inclined to write

00:48:41.270 --> 00:48:45.030
from an autobiographical perspective

00:48:45.030 --> 00:48:48.993
and to be very transparent
about my thoughts and my life.

00:48:51.094 --> 00:48:55.180
So I think that has definitely affected

00:48:56.070 --> 00:49:00.060
what I've been writing so
far, which is both poetry,

00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:02.120
some poetry and prose,

00:49:02.120 --> 00:49:05.967
where I've been comfortable in the eye of,

00:49:05.967 --> 00:49:10.967
"Minor Feelings" that
I've continued on with,

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:14.630
but I think I'm also more interested

00:49:14.630 --> 00:49:18.490
in writing about other people's lives

00:49:19.881 --> 00:49:22.530
and so I think in that way,

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:24.480
it's kind of a pivot to where,

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:28.000
it's less perhaps focused
on me and my ideas

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:30.630
about this country, my
perspective on this country

00:49:30.630 --> 00:49:33.550
and what I'm reading and my personal life

00:49:33.550 --> 00:49:38.260
and more pivot to other people's lives.

00:49:38.260 --> 00:49:41.029
- So someone in the audience wants to know

00:49:41.029 --> 00:49:43.570
if you would comment at all on

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:48.840
the college admissions lawsuits,
that Yale is now facing

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:53.840
and that are raised by questions
of Asian-American quotas

00:49:57.320 --> 00:49:59.160
and things like that.

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:00.530
My understanding is that in fact,

00:50:00.530 --> 00:50:03.580
most Asian groups on campus,

00:50:03.580 --> 00:50:06.340
have been supportive
of affirmative action,

00:50:06.340 --> 00:50:09.750
but I'm wondering if
you have any comment on

00:50:09.750 --> 00:50:13.230
where that puts
Asian-Americans and the debate

00:50:13.230 --> 00:50:15.703
within the Asian-American
community about that.

00:50:16.760 --> 00:50:18.510
- What's the update?

00:50:18.510 --> 00:50:20.020
I haven't been following.

00:50:20.020 --> 00:50:25.020
- Asians are now bringing
a lawsuit against Yale now.

00:50:25.631 --> 00:50:29.093
It seems to me with the new Supreme Court,

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:33.090
they're liable to throw
out affirmative action.

00:50:33.090 --> 00:50:34.642
I don't know.

00:50:34.642 --> 00:50:37.240
So it was defeated with Harvard,

00:50:37.240 --> 00:50:39.640
but there's another one
that's coming up to Yale

00:50:39.640 --> 00:50:41.830
and I'm sure they're gonna keep pushing.

00:50:41.830 --> 00:50:45.290
- Oh, well I'm all for affirmative action.

00:50:45.290 --> 00:50:48.050
I think that's horrifying that we would,

00:50:48.050 --> 00:50:49.282
Asian-Americans would enable

00:50:49.282 --> 00:50:54.282
the kind of federal overthrowing
of affirmative action.

00:50:54.390 --> 00:50:55.920
I think we need to stop that.

00:50:55.920 --> 00:50:57.390
That's not right.

00:50:57.390 --> 00:50:59.943
I think, you know, it's hard.

00:51:01.770 --> 00:51:04.340
Again, this is another kind
of a balancing act, right?

00:51:04.340 --> 00:51:06.693
Because it's like, we
need affirmative action.

00:51:07.813 --> 00:51:11.541
Asian-Americans have benefited
from affirmative action.

00:51:11.541 --> 00:51:14.720
You cannot deny that.

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:16.770
I have benefited from affirmative action.

00:51:16.770 --> 00:51:18.470
I mean, like I was like listening to

00:51:18.470 --> 00:51:20.070
Young Jean Lee, this playwright,

00:51:20.944 --> 00:51:22.120
wrote this op-ed for the New York Times.

00:51:22.120 --> 00:51:26.650
She was saying, "I as a
playwright, I became successful."

00:51:26.650 --> 00:51:28.370
I think she's doing herself a disservice.

00:51:28.370 --> 00:51:31.270
She became successful,
'cause she's very talented,

00:51:31.270 --> 00:51:32.521
but she's like,

00:51:32.521 --> 00:51:35.950
"I became successful, because
of affirmative action.

00:51:35.950 --> 00:51:38.320
There were no other
Asian-Americans around."

00:51:38.320 --> 00:51:43.320
It's like and also we
came to this country,

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:47.060
because of the Civil Rights Movement,

00:51:47.060 --> 00:51:49.590
because the Civil Rights Movement

00:51:49.590 --> 00:51:52.330
was making America look bad

00:51:52.330 --> 00:51:55.970
and look racist and xenophobic.

00:51:55.970 --> 00:51:57.630
So that was part of the reason why

00:51:57.630 --> 00:52:00.233
they opened their doors in 1965.

00:52:01.430 --> 00:52:03.150
So we are part of that legacy.

00:52:03.150 --> 00:52:06.310
For us to kind of, to undermine that

00:52:06.310 --> 00:52:11.310
and to kind of just
overthrow affirmative action,

00:52:13.450 --> 00:52:17.500
is just a betrayal.

00:52:17.500 --> 00:52:18.570
I think it's a betrayal,

00:52:18.570 --> 00:52:21.857
a real betrayal of what we're doing.

00:52:21.857 --> 00:52:26.263
It's a prime example of
how we are used as a wedge,

00:52:27.330 --> 00:52:28.320
first of all

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:31.177
and also how we-
- Asian-Americans

00:52:31.177 --> 00:52:33.170
who are bringing the lawsuit,

00:52:33.170 --> 00:52:35.930
it's just being done, in the name of,

00:52:35.930 --> 00:52:37.960
so it's definitely they're being used,

00:52:37.960 --> 00:52:39.040
in this sense.
- We're being used.

00:52:39.040 --> 00:52:40.690
- Yeah, yeah.
- We're definitely,

00:52:40.690 --> 00:52:41.830
and we are being used,

00:52:41.830 --> 00:52:43.770
we're being used as a pawn.

00:52:43.770 --> 00:52:46.293
Asian-Americans need
to fight against that,

00:52:47.300 --> 00:52:48.890
really fight against this,

00:52:48.890 --> 00:52:50.880
'cause I think, I've had enough.

00:52:50.880 --> 00:52:52.247
We Asian-American have to say,

00:52:52.247 --> 00:52:56.047
"Enough! We will not be used
as pawns for white supremacy."

00:52:57.536 --> 00:53:00.210
I also am understanding of the fact

00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:02.620
that there has been a
lot of discrimination

00:53:02.620 --> 00:53:06.770
and that we are, that
Asian-Americans are not,

00:53:06.770 --> 00:53:08.963
our subjectivities are not recognized,

00:53:10.150 --> 00:53:13.610
by getting rid of affirmative
action, is not the answer.

00:53:13.610 --> 00:53:15.830
I mean, of course what
needs to be addressed

00:53:15.830 --> 00:53:20.830
is class more than anything

00:53:21.200 --> 00:53:24.160
and what needs to be
addressed is the legacy,

00:53:24.160 --> 00:53:26.219
legacy as affirmative action.

00:53:26.219 --> 00:53:28.368
I don't know, I don't have the numbers,

00:53:28.368 --> 00:53:31.927
but let's look at the racial
demographic of the legacy,

00:53:31.927 --> 00:53:34.640
the people, the kids who are legacy.

00:53:34.640 --> 00:53:39.210
Like Jared Kushner, being a
very good example of that.

00:53:39.210 --> 00:53:44.210
- Right, or athletic recruits,
for sports like sailing.

00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:46.750
Yeah, I have nothing
against people who sail,

00:53:46.750 --> 00:53:50.023
but that is affirmative action
for rich people (laughs).

00:53:50.023 --> 00:53:52.130
- Yeah, it is affirmative
action to rich people.

00:53:52.130 --> 00:53:56.023
I just saw that one sort of graph where,

00:53:56.023 --> 00:54:00.140
there was SAT scores were
proportional to family income.

00:54:00.140 --> 00:54:05.140
So if your family income
was like $200,000 or more,

00:54:05.560 --> 00:54:08.010
then you had the highest SAT scores

00:54:08.010 --> 00:54:11.370
and then it just went down
from that and everything.

00:54:11.370 --> 00:54:12.490
That's what

00:54:12.490 --> 00:54:13.323
(banging)
needs to be addressed.

00:54:13.323 --> 00:54:14.460
We are looking somewhere else.

00:54:14.460 --> 00:54:16.700
By taking this to the Supreme Court,

00:54:16.700 --> 00:54:20.010
is only enabling this racial hierarchy,

00:54:20.010 --> 00:54:21.460
that we are already in.

00:54:21.460 --> 00:54:23.790
- Yeah, so here's I think probably

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:26.297
our last question from the audience.

00:54:26.297 --> 00:54:28.850
"So you write about in the book

00:54:28.850 --> 00:54:31.470
being kind of opposed to writing about

00:54:31.470 --> 00:54:34.409
Asian-American identity issues,

00:54:34.409 --> 00:54:37.360
but of course, the whole
book is about that.

00:54:37.360 --> 00:54:40.690
So what led you to
finally change your mind

00:54:40.690 --> 00:54:43.640
about writing about
Asian-American identity?"

00:54:43.640 --> 00:54:46.300
Which actually links up
with the last question

00:54:46.300 --> 00:54:47.830
I had kind of wanted to ask you,

00:54:47.830 --> 00:54:50.500
which is, have you changed your thoughts

00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:52.850
on saying we in the book.

00:54:52.850 --> 00:54:54.977
You write about, you have a hard time

00:54:54.977 --> 00:54:57.970
talking collectively,
in a collective voice,

00:54:57.970 --> 00:54:58.830
about Asian-Americans

00:54:58.830 --> 00:55:00.930
and I'm wondering if that changed for you?

00:55:02.422 --> 00:55:03.255
- I think it did.

00:55:03.255 --> 00:55:07.680
I think I've been very
heartened by and really happy.

00:55:07.680 --> 00:55:10.850
I thought I was afraid
about this book coming out.

00:55:10.850 --> 00:55:12.960
I was afraid that no one would read it,

00:55:12.960 --> 00:55:13.973
no one would care.

00:55:15.350 --> 00:55:17.470
Because it's about Asian-American identity

00:55:17.470 --> 00:55:20.140
and it's kind of a muted subject

00:55:20.140 --> 00:55:21.498
and I don't think that,

00:55:21.498 --> 00:55:23.853
but I think that's how
it's treated, approached.

00:55:25.440 --> 00:55:28.320
I've been really, really heartened by,

00:55:28.320 --> 00:55:31.630
the sheer number of readers

00:55:31.630 --> 00:55:34.270
who have told me that they've felt seen

00:55:34.270 --> 00:55:37.005
and that they recognize
themselves in the book.

00:55:37.005 --> 00:55:41.213
It's not just East Asians.

00:55:41.213 --> 00:55:42.550
It's a lot of East Asians,

00:55:42.550 --> 00:55:44.582
it's also a lot of people

00:55:44.582 --> 00:55:48.550
from other Asian-American demographic

00:55:49.954 --> 00:55:53.333
and also non-Asian minorities too.

00:55:54.250 --> 00:55:59.250
So I guess I feel more
emboldened to say we,

00:56:00.380 --> 00:56:05.353
but I would still have
the caveat (laughs) there.

00:56:07.090 --> 00:56:09.210
I think what's really
important to understand,

00:56:09.210 --> 00:56:12.710
is that there are some,

00:56:12.710 --> 00:56:14.637
there was this one
reviewer who was saying,

00:56:14.637 --> 00:56:18.050
"But well, she doesn't really say",

00:56:18.050 --> 00:56:19.360
was dissatisfied with the book,

00:56:19.360 --> 00:56:20.950
because I don't really talk about

00:56:20.950 --> 00:56:24.200
what the Asian-American experience is.

00:56:24.200 --> 00:56:25.890
I just talk about sort of

00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:28.570
our relations to white supremacy

00:56:30.874 --> 00:56:35.400
and our fraught relationship
with African-Americans.

00:56:36.410 --> 00:56:37.243
But they're like,

00:56:37.243 --> 00:56:39.700
"But what about, what is
Asian-American experience?"

00:56:39.700 --> 00:56:42.277
I'm like, "I can't answer that question."

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:48.503
Asian-American is a coalition
of different nationalities.

00:56:52.940 --> 00:56:55.620
So what I'm trying to,

00:56:55.620 --> 00:56:57.807
I guess why I wrote that book to say,

00:56:57.807 --> 00:57:00.120
"Look, let's embrace the fact that

00:57:00.120 --> 00:57:03.820
we are a coalition and
that as a coalition,

00:57:03.820 --> 00:57:07.210
we need to kind of also
try to make bridges with

00:57:07.210 --> 00:57:12.210
other people of color, or
people who are marginalized

00:57:13.770 --> 00:57:17.436
and to build upon those coalitions,

00:57:17.436 --> 00:57:21.787
to fight what this country has become now,

00:57:23.770 --> 00:57:26.177
the fallen empire that is America."

00:57:27.650 --> 00:57:32.650
I can't tell you what the
Asian-American experience is.

00:57:32.910 --> 00:57:34.080
I can only tell you,

00:57:34.080 --> 00:57:38.650
I mean, the book is my own perspective

00:57:38.650 --> 00:57:40.460
on both the Asian-American experience

00:57:40.460 --> 00:57:44.960
and also my perspective
as an Asian-American woman

00:57:44.960 --> 00:57:46.790
on this country.

00:57:46.790 --> 00:57:49.789
That was always the thesis of the book.

00:57:49.789 --> 00:57:52.953
To answer the person's question,

00:57:54.824 --> 00:57:58.620
I think it was, I always read about race

00:57:58.620 --> 00:58:00.929
and it was something that

00:58:00.929 --> 00:58:03.530
I was interested in for a long time,

00:58:03.530 --> 00:58:05.450
to write a book about
Asian-American identity.

00:58:05.450 --> 00:58:06.640
I think it was just because

00:58:06.640 --> 00:58:08.511
I felt so uncomfortable about it.

00:58:08.511 --> 00:58:11.303
I like to make my life hard by,

00:58:12.650 --> 00:58:13.930
I'm like a moth to a flame.

00:58:13.930 --> 00:58:15.370
I like to kind of write about

00:58:15.370 --> 00:58:18.990
what I am scared of and what
I feel uncomfortable about,

00:58:18.990 --> 00:58:20.410
because I'm like, "Oh well, what is there?

00:58:20.410 --> 00:58:22.147
Why do I feel uncomfortable?"

00:58:26.190 --> 00:58:28.090
But I've said this in many interviews,

00:58:29.570 --> 00:58:31.840
It's also because I became a mother

00:58:31.840 --> 00:58:32.673
and I was like,

00:58:32.673 --> 00:58:36.227
"What kind of country do I
want my daughter to live in?"

00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:39.240
I don't want her to have

00:58:39.240 --> 00:58:41.010
the same kind of internalized racism,

00:58:41.010 --> 00:58:42.640
that I had when I was a child.

00:58:42.640 --> 00:58:44.050
I wanna protect her from that.

00:58:44.050 --> 00:58:49.030
I want her to walk freely in this world.

00:58:50.570 --> 00:58:53.332
So I wrote a book that I hoped was

00:58:53.332 --> 00:58:57.020
a step towards that, in the world.

00:58:57.020 --> 00:58:58.580
- Well, that's a great answer.

00:58:58.580 --> 00:59:00.880
Unfortunately, we are out of time.

00:59:00.880 --> 00:59:03.820
I feel like we could
go on talking all day.

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:05.460
Luckily, we know each other

00:59:05.460 --> 00:59:07.360
and I can keep asking
you all the questions,

00:59:07.360 --> 00:59:09.390
that I didn't get to ask you.

00:59:09.390 --> 00:59:11.830
But for those of you who, unfortunately,

00:59:11.830 --> 00:59:13.240
don't know you, I think that,

00:59:13.240 --> 00:59:16.410
I just really advise
that you get a hold of

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:20.060
a copy as soon as possible
and read the book.

00:59:20.060 --> 00:59:23.600
So Cathy, thank you so
much for talking to us.

00:59:23.600 --> 00:59:28.600
This was a fantastic gift to
us and to the Yale community.

00:59:29.130 --> 00:59:30.500
I'm really grateful.

00:59:30.500 --> 00:59:33.470
So good luck with
everything and thank you.

00:59:33.470 --> 00:59:35.491
- Thank you, this was a real pleasure.

00:59:35.491 --> 00:59:36.448
Thank you, Mauri.

00:59:36.448 --> 00:59:38.410
I hope we get to talk more later.

00:59:38.410 --> 00:59:40.010
- [Maurice] Terrific, thank you.

